| **1** | **Warner Bros. Studio Tour – “The Making of Harry Potter”** | Leavesden (≈ 20 min by train from London Euston) | Walk the real sets (Great Hall, Diagon Alley, Hogwarts Express, Forbidden Forest, etc.), see props, costumes, and learn the film‑making secrets. | **£47 – £55** (standard ticket; prices vary a little by season) | **Under £100** and the definitive Harry‑Potter experience. |
| 2 | **Harry Potter Walking Tour – “Wizarding London”** | Central London (various start points, e.g., King’s Cross) | Guided 2‑hour stroll to filming locations: Platform 9¾/King’s Cross, Leadenhall Market (Diagon Alley), Millennium Bridge (Deathly Hallows), etc. | **£25 – £35** | Small‑group, easy on the wallet, and you stay right in the city. |
| 3 | **Harry Potter & The Cursed Child** (West End) | Palace Theatre, London | See the award‑winning stage play that continues the story of Harry, Ron, Hermione and their children. | **£45 – £120** (most standard seats are under £100; premium seats can exceed it) | Choose a standard‑price seat and you stay under £100 while enjoying a magical night out. |
| 4 | **Harry Potter Pop‑Up Experiences** (seasonal, e.g., “The Wizarding World of Harry Potter” pop‑up) | Various London venues (often in the summer/fall) | Interactive rooms, photo‑ops, themed food & drink, mini‑games. | **£15 – £30** | Fun, short, and definitely budget‑friendly. |
| 5 | **Harry Potter‑themed Escape Rooms** (e.g., “The Ministry of Magic Escape”) | Central London (several locations) | Solve puzzles in a Hogwarts‑style setting to “escape” before time runs out. | **£20 – £30** per person | Great for a family activity that’s under £100. |
| 6 | **Harry Potter Self‑Guided Audio Tour (App‑based)** | Downloadable app, you roam at your own pace | Audio commentary and maps that point out film locations across the city. | **≈ £10** for the app purchase | Cheapest way to see the sites, perfect if you prefer to explore on your own schedule. |

\*Prices are the typical adult rates you’ll see on the official websites or major booking platforms (e.g., Viator, GetYourGuide, the venue’s own site). They can vary slightly with season, promotions, or “early‑bird” discounts, but all listed figures are well‑under the £100 ceiling.

---

## How to book the **Warner Bros. Studio Tour** (the first option that meets your budget)

1. **Go to the official site:** https://www.wbstudiotour.co.uk/  
2. Click **“Book Now”** and select **“Standard Adult”** (you’ll see the price displayed as £47‑£55 depending on the date).  
3. Choose a date and time slot (the tour is very popular, so it’s best to book at least 2‑3 weeks ahead).  
4. Add any optional extras you like (e.g., a Butterbeer drink, a souvenir wand‑style key‑chain, or the “Wizarding World” photo‑package). Even with one or two extras the total stays comfortably under £100.  
5. Complete the payment and you’ll receive an e‑ticket with a QR code to scan on the day of your visit.

**Getting there:**  
- Take a **train from London Euston to Watford Junction** (≈ 20 min).  
- From Watford Junction, a **shuttle bus** (included with your ticket) runs directly to the studio.  
- The whole trip is about 45 minutes each way, leaving plenty of time to explore the tour (≈ 3 hours) and still have the rest of the day for other London sights.

---

## Quick tips for a smooth Harry‑Potter day in London

| Tip | Why it helps |
|-----|--------------|
| **Book online in advance** | The studio tour and West‑End show sell out quickly, especially on weekends and school holidays. |
| **Travel off‑peak** | Trains to Watford Junction are cheaper and less crowded outside rush hour. |
| **Bring a reusable water bottle** | You’ll be walking a lot on the walking tours; many locations have water fountains. |
| **Check age‑related pricing** | Children (under 12) usually get a discounted rate on the studio tour (£38 – £40) and on most walking tours. |
| **Combine activities** | You can do the studio tour in the morning, then a quick Harry‑Potter walking tour in the afternoon (the two are in different parts of the city, but both are doable in a single day). |

---

### Bottom line
The **Warner Bros. Studio Tour** is the first Harry‑Potter‑themed experience that fits comfortably under your £100 budget, and it offers the most immersive, “inside‑the‑movies” feel. If you prefer to stay strictly within central London, the **Harry Potter Walking Tour** is a fantastic, budget‑friendly alternative at just £25‑£35.

Let me know if you’d like more details on any of the other options (e.g., ticket links for the West‑End show, recommended walking‑tour operators, or family‑friendly pricing), and I’ll be happy to help you plan the perfect magical day for your child!

